# Spec-driven development

This project is configured to use **Spec-Kit** for spec-driven development (SDD).

!!! warning "Doc freshness"
    Content here was written against **Spec-Kit's integration architecture**.
    The tool is rolling-release — flag names and skill or command sets drift
    between releases. **When this doc disagrees with the CLI, trust the CLI
    and the upstream docs:**

    - Spec-Kit: <https://github.com/github/spec-kit> · [Installation Guide](https://github.github.io/spec-kit/installation.html)
    - Run `specify --help` and `specify integration list` locally for the authoritative options.

## When to reach for SDD

SDD pays off when the cost of the agent guessing wrong is higher than the cost
of writing the spec.

| Task | Use SDD? |
|---|---|
| New feature touching >3 files | Yes |
| Schema migration / backfill | Yes (always) |
| Refactor with API changes | Yes |
| Pipeline rebuild (dbt, Airflow, …) | Yes |
| New RAG or eval pipeline | Yes |
| Bug fix in 1–2 files | No — go straight to `/plan` |
| Lint / docstring / config tweak | No |
| Spike / prototype | No |

## Spec-Kit (configured)

Spec-Kit turns a feature description into a set of reviewable artefacts: a
specification, implementation plan, supporting research and data model, and an
ordered task list. Its analysis step checks consistency before implementation.

### One-time install

```bash
uv tool install specify-cli --from git+https://github.com/github/spec-kit.git@{{ tool_versions.spec_kit_ref }}
specify version
specify integration list
```

### Initialise inside this project

> The Copier `_tasks` step already runs this for you when the CLI is on
> `PATH` during the initial `copier copy`. Copier updates never rerun bootstrap
> tasks. The block below is the manual fallback and the migration path for a
> project generated before multi-agent integration support.

```bash
specify init . --here --integration {{ sdd_install_agent }} --force --ignore-agent-tools
{% if sdd_install_agent != 'opencode' -%}
{% if 'claude_code' in coding_agents and sdd_install_agent != 'claude' -%}
specify integration install claude
{% endif -%}
{% if 'codex' in coding_agents and sdd_install_agent != 'codex' -%}
specify integration install codex
{% endif -%}
{% if 'pi' in coding_agents and sdd_install_agent != 'pi' -%}
specify integration install pi
{% endif -%}
{%- endif %}
```

Run `specify integration list` to see the integrations supported by your
installed CLI. This template can initialise `claude`, `codex`, `pi`, or
`opencode`; Aider is not currently offered by Spec-Kit as an integration.
`--ignore-agent-tools` allows setup before the selected agent is installed on
the host; it does not disable checks inside later Spec-Kit workflows.
Pi does not have built-in MCP support, so Spec-Kit's `taskstoissues` integration
requires a separately reviewed Pi extension.

### Multiple coding agents

Spec-Kit records one **default integration** and any number of additional
installed integrations. This project's default is `{{ sdd_install_agent }}`.
Copier also installs every selected integration that Spec-Kit declares safe for
multi-installation: Claude Code, Codex, and Pi use separate command or skill
paths and can work from the same `.specify/` and `specs/` content.

```bash
specify integration status
specify integration use <installed-key>  # changes the default; does not uninstall others
```

{% if 'opencode' in coding_agents -%}
OpenCode is selected for this project, but Spec-Kit does not currently declare
its integration multi-install-safe. {% if sdd_install_agent == 'opencode' %}It
therefore remains the only automatically installed integration.{% else %}Copier
therefore leaves it out of the automatic multi-agent installation.{% endif %}
To accept Spec-Kit's warning and install another integration alongside the
existing set, opt in explicitly:

```bash
{% if sdd_install_agent == 'opencode' -%}
# Replace `claude` with another selected integration as needed.
specify integration install claude --force
{% else -%}
specify integration install opencode --force
{% endif -%}
```

Review the generated agent paths before committing them; `--force` here
acknowledges Spec-Kit's multi-install safety check.
{% endif -%}

### Memory architecture

| File | Contents | Read by |
|---|---|---|
| `.specify/memory/constitution.md` | Architectural principles, non-negotiable | Spec-Kit (every phase) |
| `AGENTS.md` (repo root) | Executor rules, QA commands | All agents |

On conflict, precedence is **Constitution > spec.md > plan.md > tasks.md > code**.

### What gets generated

```
.specify/
├── memory/constitution.md      # architectural principles (non-negotiable)
├── scripts/                    # env validation & helpers
└── templates/                  # markdown skeletons
specs/
└── <feature>/                  # spec.md, plan.md, research.md, data-model.md, tasks.md
```

### Workflow

The full workflow is:

```
constitution → specify → clarify → plan → checklist → tasks → analyze → implement
```

For a smaller, well-understood feature, use:

```
specify → plan → tasks → implement
```

The invocation syntax depends on the selected integration. Use the commands or
skills installed by Spec-Kit: Claude Code, Pi, and OpenCode expose
`/speckit.*` commands, while Codex exposes `$speckit-*` skills.

### Update later

```bash
uv tool install specify-cli --force --from git+https://github.com/github/spec-kit.git@{{ tool_versions.spec_kit_ref }}
specify integration upgrade {{ sdd_install_agent }}
{% if sdd_install_agent != 'opencode' -%}
{% if 'claude_code' in coding_agents and sdd_install_agent != 'claude' -%}
specify integration upgrade claude
{% endif -%}
{% if 'codex' in coding_agents and sdd_install_agent != 'codex' -%}
specify integration upgrade codex
{% endif -%}
{% if 'pi' in coding_agents and sdd_install_agent != 'pi' -%}
specify integration upgrade pi
{% endif -%}
{%- endif %}
```

Upgrade each installed integration separately. The manifest-aware upgrade path
preserves locally modified integration files and does not touch specifications
or the constitution unless you explicitly force it.

## Verify the setup

```bash
specify version
specify integration list
specify integration status
ls .specify/                   # memory/, scripts/, templates/
ls specs/                      # one directory per feature
```

Smoke-test in your agent session with the installed constitution command or
skill. If it is recognised, the setup is wired correctly.

## SDD and the workflow skills

The nine workflow skills in `skills/` stay relevant — SDD does not replace
them, it deepens the ones that matter most:

| Workflow skill | With Spec-Kit |
|---|---|
| `/spec` | Maps to Spec-Kit's specify + clarify phases — a multi-artifact contract instead of one file |
| `/plan` | Maps to Spec-Kit's plan + tasks phases |
| `/test` | Driven from the spec's acceptance criteria as before |
| `/delegate` | Bundle the spec artefacts directly into the brief |
| `/review` | Verify the diff against `spec.md` + `tasks.md` |

## Anti-patterns

- **Verbose specs for trivial tasks.** A 16-criterion spec for a one-line
  bugfix is cargo-cult. Skip SDD for those.
- **Set-and-forget specs.** On multi-week initiatives, re-align the spec to
  reality after each iteration. Specs that drift become misleading docs.
- **Mixing SDD frameworks.** Keep one framework as the project contract.

